for always, as ones about to mount the chariot, the two were like,
and with the breath of them Eumelus’ back and the two broad shoulders
were warmed; for upon him, having laid down their heads, the two flew.

And now he would either have driven past or made it disputed,
if at the son of Tydeus Phoebus Apollo had not borne a grudge,
who from his hands struck the shining whip.

And not did Apollo escape Athena, as he cheated
the Tydeus’-son, and very swiftly she rushed after the shepherd of the people,
and gave him the whip, and put might into the horses;

and he himself from the chariot beside the wheel was rolled out,
and was scraped round at the elbows and mouth and nostrils,
and he was battered in the forehead at the brows;

Indeed with those I do not at all bid you to quarrel,
the horses of the Tydeus’-son, wise-minded, to which Athena
now held out speed and upon him set glory;

but overtake the horses of Atreus’-son, and do not be left behind,
swiftly, lest disgrace upon you two pour down
Aethe, being female;

And as great as the range of a discus hurled from the shoulder is,
which a vigorous man has let go, testing his youthful strength,
so far the two ran on;

Other horses seem to me to be in front,
and another charioteer shows himself; and the mares, I suppose, right there
were hindered in the plain, which were out there the stronger;
